putnam-2005-b4.body:
Extend the definition to $m,n\ge0$: $f(m,0) = 1$ (only the empty tuple) and $f(0,n) = 1$ (only the zero tuple). Let $S_{m,n}$ be the set of tuples counted by $f(m,n)$, for $m,n\ge1$. Split it into three classes:
- $|x_1|+\cdots+|x_n| < m$: these are exactly the elements of $S_{m-1,n}$;
- sum $= m$ and $x_n\ge0$: dropping $x_n$ gives an element of $S_{m,n-1}$, and conversely any element of $S_{m,n-1}$ extends uniquely by $x_n = m - (|x_1|+\cdots+|x_{n-1}|)\ge0$;
- sum $= m$ and $x_n<0$: dropping $x_n$ gives an element of $S_{m-1,n-1}$ (the remaining sum is $m - |x_n|\le m-1$), and conversely any element of $S_{m-1,n-1}$ extends uniquely by $x_n = -(m - \text{sum}) < 0$.

So $f(m,n) = f(m-1,n) + f(m,n-1) + f(m-1,n-1)$ for $m,n\ge1$. The recurrence and the boundary values are symmetric under $m\leftrightarrow n$, so by induction on $m+n$, $f(m,n) = f(n,m)$. ∎

(Equivalently, $\sum_{m,n\ge0}f(m,n)x^my^n = \frac{1}{1-x-y-xy}$, which is symmetric.)
